1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,540
Twenty three people in a room.

2
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:03,080
Bet me two of them share a birthday.

3
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:04,980
Most people take the other side.

4
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:09,200
They lose that bet about half the time, and I want to show you exactly how I know.

5
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:13,520
Hi, I'm Tanmay Kulkarni, in for Humanitarians AI.

6
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:20,340
Three things could change that odds figure: how many people are in the group, how uneven the birthdays are, and how many days they have to land on.

7
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:22,500
I'll change each one and watch the number.

8
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:27,140
One of them barely matters, one matters the way you'd expect, and one decides almost everything.

9
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:28,480
Hold a guess about which.

10
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:32,420
The trick is to stop counting people and count pairs.

11
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:35,420
Twenty three people make two hundred and fifty three pairs.

12
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:41,280
Each pair is a small chance, and two hundred and fifty three small chances pile up faster than your gut expects.

13
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:43,680
First change: the people.

14
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,940
I run four hundred thousand random groups of twenty three and count how often a pair shares a day.

15
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:49,680
Fifty point seven percent.

16
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:51,180
The exact calculation agrees.

17
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:52,220
Now add people.

18
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:53,780
With ten, about twelve percent.

19
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:55,340
With thirty, seventy one percent.

20
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:56,800
With fifty, ninety seven percent.

21
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,280
By fifty seven people, past ninety nine percent.

22
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:01,520
More people, more odds, just as you'd expect.

23
00:01:02,567 --> 00:01:04,287
One thing the claim does not say.

24
00:01:04,307 --> 00:01:06,087
It says some pair, not a pair with you.

25
00:01:06,307 --> 00:01:11,067
For somebody to share your birthday, you need two hundred and fifty three other people to reach even odds.

26
00:01:11,367 --> 00:01:14,567
With twenty two others in the room, your chance is under six percent.

27
00:01:14,467 --> 00:01:16,327
Same crowd, a different question.

28
00:01:17,433 --> 00:01:19,313
Second change: how even the days are.

29
00:01:19,393 --> 00:01:21,813
So far every day was equally likely, and that's false.

30
00:01:22,413 --> 00:01:24,593
I used federal counts of babies born each day.

31
00:01:24,713 --> 00:01:30,573
They come from the National Center for Health Statistics and the Social Security Administration, and cover two thousand to two thousand fourteen.

32
00:01:31,073 --> 00:01:33,593
The busiest day runs about twelve percent above average.

33
00:01:33,973 --> 00:01:38,913
February twenty ninth runs at about a quarter of a normal day, because it only exists one year in four.

34
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:41,960
So I ran it again on the real calendar.

35
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:45,680
Fifty point eight percent, against fifty point seven for even days.

36
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:50,080
Uneven days can only raise the odds, and they did, by a tenth of a point at most.

37
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:50,900
That's tiny.

38
00:01:52,067 --> 00:01:59,267
About ninety six percent of days sit within ten percent of average, so the real calendar is close enough to even that the model barely notices.

39
00:01:59,867 --> 00:02:01,407
The wrong assumption was forgiving.

40
00:02:01,827 --> 00:02:05,907
Keep that in mind, because the third change breaks the model somewhere else entirely.

41
00:02:07,067 --> 00:02:09,467
Third change: how many days there are to land on.

42
00:02:09,747 --> 00:02:10,207
Squeeze it.

43
00:02:10,347 --> 00:02:15,247
Not twenty three people from anywhere in the year, but twenty three people all born inside the same ninety days.

44
00:02:15,627 --> 00:02:16,107
Ninety five point four percent.

45
00:02:17,367 --> 00:02:22,227
Same group size, a quarter of the calendar, and the odds go from a coin flip to nearly certain.

46
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:24,573
Let me slide the window.

47
00:02:24,473 --> 00:02:25,673
A full year: fifty point eight.

48
00:02:26,073 --> 00:02:27,293
Half a year: seventy seven.

49
00:02:27,633 --> 00:02:28,753
Ninety days: ninety five.

50
00:02:28,973 --> 00:02:30,313
Sixty days: ninety nine.

51
00:02:30,513 --> 00:02:32,653
Born in the same month: better than ninety nine point nine percent.

52
00:02:33,493 --> 00:02:38,053
And with a window of seven days, twenty three people can't avoid sharing, there aren't enough days.

53
00:02:39,233 --> 00:02:42,473
Flip it around and ask how many people you need for even odds.

54
00:02:42,653 --> 00:02:44,073
Across a whole year, twenty three.

55
00:02:44,473 --> 00:02:45,713
Ninety day window: twelve.

56
00:02:45,853 --> 00:02:46,833
Sixty days: ten.

57
00:02:47,253 --> 00:02:48,653
A thirty day window: seven.

58
00:02:48,933 --> 00:02:49,893
A fortnight: five.

59
00:02:50,093 --> 00:02:50,753
A week: four.

60
00:02:51,867 --> 00:02:53,227
Here is what I almost missed.

61
00:02:53,367 --> 00:02:56,127
Does the uneven calendar still matter inside those windows?

62
00:02:56,447 --> 00:02:57,867
A tenth of a point at most, again.

63
00:02:58,227 --> 00:02:59,287
Seasons weren't the story.

64
00:02:59,687 --> 00:03:03,107
Of the three things, the head count moves the odds the way you'd expect.

65
00:03:03,247 --> 00:03:05,347
The evenness of the days barely moves them.

66
00:03:05,487 --> 00:03:07,687
And the number of days decides almost everything.

67
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:10,253
Look at those head-counts again.

68
00:03:10,333 --> 00:03:12,413
Twenty three for three hundred and sixty five days.

69
00:03:12,513 --> 00:03:13,233
Twelve for ninety.

70
00:03:13,333 --> 00:03:14,273
Seven for thirty.

71
00:03:14,353 --> 00:03:15,133
They follow a rule.

72
00:03:15,213 --> 00:03:16,493
Take the square root of the days.

73
00:03:16,773 --> 00:03:17,673
Multiply by one point two.

74
00:03:18,273 --> 00:03:19,093
Add a half, and round.

75
00:03:19,193 --> 00:03:20,213
A year gives twenty three.

76
00:03:20,333 --> 00:03:21,493
Ninety days gives twelve.

77
00:03:21,613 --> 00:03:22,733
Thirty days gives seven.

78
00:03:22,793 --> 00:03:23,833
A week gives four.

79
00:03:23,973 --> 00:03:28,913
It is right for every window in this film, and never more than one person off anywhere from two slots to two thousand.

80
00:03:30,233 --> 00:03:33,333
A fair objection: classmates are all about the same age.

81
00:03:33,613 --> 00:03:39,933
But a school year with a cutoff date still contains every day of the calendar once, so it behaves like the full year, about fifty point eight percent.

82
00:03:40,893 --> 00:03:44,693
The squeeze only happens if the group is pinned to a narrow stretch of days.

83
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:47,880
Now use it on something that isn't a birthday.

84
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:49,400
Birth months: twelve slots.

85
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:51,740
The square root of twelve is about three and a half.

86
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:54,200
Times one point two, plus a half, rounds to five.

87
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:59,400
So five people are enough for even odds that two share a birth month, if every month were equally likely.

88
00:03:59,740 --> 00:04:01,460
Four people give forty three percent.

89
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:02,600
Five give sixty two.

90
00:04:03,767 --> 00:04:04,447
Your turn.

91
00:04:04,467 --> 00:04:07,147
Everyone in a class picks a random number from one to fifty.

92
00:04:07,467 --> 00:04:09,507
How many people before two probably match?

93
00:04:09,687 --> 00:04:10,327
Use the rule.

94
00:04:11,187 --> 00:04:13,107
A tempting wrong answer is half the slots, twenty five.

95
00:04:13,727 --> 00:04:15,227
Pause, work it out, and I'll say.

96
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:26,000
The root of fifty is about seven.

97
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:28,580
Times one point two, plus a half, rounds to nine.

98
00:04:29,020 --> 00:04:30,800
Nine people give fifty three percent.

99
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:31,920
Eight give forty five.

100
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:32,760
Not twenty five.

101
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:36,160
The slots set the scale, and their square root sets the crowd.

102
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:42,000
Next time someone quotes odds that two things match, ask how many slots they could fall into.

103
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:44,860
The square root of that number is about how many it takes.

104
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:47,880
Tanmay Kulkarni, in for Humanitarians AI.

